nsure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330792/field-5" :answer-field/id #uuid "06848516-faef-548d-ae28-e0d5e7be166b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330792/field-5/bdb344504abc3a20477be3b0061a467b51fce04e586ae596a6ef1bbf0033c4ee" :answer-field/choices ["q-330792/field-5/bdb344504abc3a20477be3b0061a467b51fce04e586ae596a6ef1bbf0033c4ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330792/field-6/c4b2fef88ba97e017ab533732894399e1b8d9a2297cc984e872e958456a350f0" :answer/id #uuid "70c10a5d-0318-5428-9e55-c68156704aff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\ln(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330792/field-6" :answer-field/id #uuid "4cb77b50-59e8-535b-a845-f5ecbfc9577e" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330792/field-6/c4b2fef88ba97e017ab533732894399e1b8d9a2297cc984e872e958456a350f0" :answer-field/choices ["q-330792/field-6/c4b2fef88ba97e017ab533732894399e1b8d9a2297cc984e872e958456a350f0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330792" :question/id #uuid "7e7a1a47-e105-53bd-b03f-663b5f4a0476" :question/math-academy-id "q-330792" :question/problem "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+t\\,{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | $3$ | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t),$ ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t),$ and ${y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-t\\,{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-t\\,{x}_{3}+3{x}_{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=3{x}_{2}-t\\,{x}_{3}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-330792/field-1" "q-330792/field-2" "q-330792/field-3" "q-330792/field-4" "q-330792/field-5" "q-330792/field-6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a7f2a0b-777f-5d63-9f3a-3b3f4a6969d8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-330792"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-