ld-5/b4ac6936776440214aaebffba73d7fd5fde48538e542fa8a6de8788032995d2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321369/field-6/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "f82fa6de-2b80-50dd-8ed0-74329d168b1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174878 :answer-field/correct "q-321369/field-6/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-321369/field-6/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-321369"] :question/problem "The third-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}-t{y}^{″}+{e}^{t}{y}^{′}=2$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} |  | $,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | $-{e}^{t}$ | {{field-5}} |  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t),$ ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t),$ and ${y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=t{y}^{″}-{e}^{t}{y}^{′}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=t{x}_{3}-{e}^{t}{x}_{2}+2.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=-{e}^{t}{x}_{2}+t{x}_{3}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{t} & t\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9042"] :question/problem "The second-order differential equation \n\n$$\ny''  - 7t y' + e^{t}y= -t\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1(t) = y(t)$ and $x_2(t) = y'(t).$ Find the function $f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1$ and $x_2.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t y' - e^{t}y - t.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny'' = 7t x_2 -e^{t}x_1 - t.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=7t{x}_{2}-{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = -e^{t}x_1 + 7t x_2 - t.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9139"] :question/problem "Consider the third-order differential equation\n\n$$\ny''' + t y' - 8 y = 1 - t^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $y = y(t).$ If\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {y}^{″}={x}_{3}(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the given differential equation can be rewritten as the matrix equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t) + \\mathbf f(t),$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/2b3ba6edd838ee516ad902008726afbca516ab15892aad0f236c5a2270261985.png)\nDetermine the matrices $A$ and $\\mathbf{f}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′} & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of $x_1, x_2$ and $x_3.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $(y'''),$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny''' = - t y' + 8 y + 1 - t^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\ny''' = - t x_2 + 8 x_1 + 1 - t^2.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=8{x}_{1}-t{x}_{2}+1-{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf x'(t) = A\\mathbf x(t) + \\mathbf f(t)$ as follows:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/5a343acd288c1f6ddc72320b4eb17d9f24d3d41adebec579e70b9ff7ba17d0bb.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070598/assets/dcb089aa64d2aeb959e6b33b03afecef75de96962a0468eb31b590ed6ecad6eb.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
